  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nModels continue to show mid/upper level ridging building into\nthe Great Lakes and Ohio Valley by early next week. High\ntemperatures reaching the upper 80s to low 90s is possible\nMonday through Wednesday, with heat indices in the mid 90s\npossible. NBM probabilities for heat indices greater than 95F\npeak on Wednesday, with elevated probs Tuesday and Thursday as\nwell. Heat headlines are not out of the question at this point,\nespecially in the western/southwestern portions of the area, but\nprobs of heat indices greater than 100F remain low at this \ntime. Heat impacts could be elevated with even 95F heat indices\ngiven the time of year. Realizing these temperatures will be\ncontingent on shower/thunderstorm activity and resultant cloud\ncover, however at this time convection is expected to be \nprimarily focused north of the area Tuesday and Wednesday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nDespite model solution convergence on building ridge into the\nGreat Lakes region next week, confidence remains low on\npotential for organized convection that could periodically\nimpact the area. A warm front will lift north into the area\nSunday, with organized convection possible upstream across the\ncentral Great Lakes sliding southeast along this front. Models\nare a bit further north with this activity than 24 hours ago,\nwhich would keep most of this activity north of the forecast\narea. The overall pattern looks to become more northwest flow\nthrough midweek vs. the more zonal mid/upper level pattern \nreflected by last night's model runs. Despite this, the best\nmid/upper flow should remain north of the area during this time,\nalthough organized convective complexes may favor a track\ntowards the area if sufficient destabilization manifests. The\nbest precip chances will be Thursday as a cold front pushes\nsouth into the area as a potent mid/upper trough/low digs\nsoutheast towards the lower Great Lakes/New England.",
